# Feature pipeline lead

Read this project's root `CLAUDE.md` or `AGENTS.md` first. Then read
`.claude/commands/feature.md` in full and execute its lead workflow from
Preflight through Report. That file is the canonical pipeline sequence; its
Claude frontmatter and `$ARGUMENTS` placeholder are syntax, not extra rules.

Apply these Codex adaptations:

- The feature request is the user's request that invoked this skill.
- Run `scripts/codex-preflight.sh` from your current sandbox before canonical
  Preflight. Verify that Codex discovers the project `planner` agent and all
  three supporting skills; a file-presence check cannot establish discovery.
  On failure, report the exact blocked operation. Request narrowly scoped
  runtime approval when needed; never switch to full access or disable approvals.
  Its authenticated API check replaces canonical Preflight's step 3 server
  probe, so `OC_SERVER` overrides and password-gated servers are handled once.
- When the canonical flow says to dispatch `planner`, spawn the project-scoped
  Codex `planner` subagent. Wait for it and use its short reply as a receipt;
  its full output belongs in `.pipeline/T-<id>.md`.
- Record **Codex planner thread id** in the task file after the first dispatch.
  Same task -> same planner thread: send corrections, verify-spec failures, and
  the one permitted spec bounce to that thread. Wait for completion before any
  follow-up; after interruption inspect its status and the state file first.
  Never start a second writer while the previous planner is active. If the
  thread is gone, record that fact and start a replacement using the task file
  and written decisions. New task -> new thread; release the thread when the
  task ends, keeping the recorded id for audit. Respect the model/reasoning
  settings in the planner file; otherwise inherit the lead's settings.
- When the canonical flow dispatches the tester: if the project defines a
  Codex `tester` agent (`.codex/agents/tester.toml`, `tester_model: codex/…`),
  spawn it, use its short reply as a receipt, and record **Codex tester thread
  id** after the first dispatch; reuse that thread only for tester retries on
  the same task — it is never an OpenCode session. Otherwise dispatch the
  OpenCode tester through `scripts/oc.sh` as the canonical flow shows.
- Use OpenCode `builder` as the implementer. The Claude `senior-dev` option is
  unavailable in a Codex-led fallback run. All review-independence rules still
  apply.
- Give every new `T-*` task its own `codex/T-<id>-<slug>` branch, created from
  an up-to-date base before any implementer runs. Branch creation is the
  lead's action — no implementer permission block grants it — and one task's
  work never lands on another task's branch. Request runtime approval for the
  precise `git switch -c` when the sandbox needs it; never widen scope to get it.
- `scripts/oc.sh` — a dispatch, `--status`, and `--interrupt` alike — needs
  the approved, narrowly scoped runtime permission to run outside the
  workspace sandbox. The OpenCode CLI opens a log under its own home directory
  before it reaches the local server, so a sandboxed call fails `EPERM` and
  reports nothing. Treat that as the sandbox, never as a dead server, and
  never broaden the sandbox, enable unrestricted network access, or put
  `.pipeline/.oc-password` on a command line to get around it. Prefer the
  wrapper over direct `opencode api` calls: it resolves server, port file and
  credential in one place.
- You have no background-watch tool. Launch every OpenCode dispatch and every
  headless Claude review with `scripts/bg-dispatch.sh start T-<id> <label> --
  <wrapper …>`, then call `scripts/bg-dispatch.sh wait T-<id> <label> <seconds>
  [--session id]` with `<seconds>` below your own command timeout, again and
  again, until it prints `finished:` (exit 0) or `wrapper-exited:` (exit 2).
  `still-running` (exit 3) means call it again — never re-dispatch over a live
  label (the script refuses anyway), and never treat a killed `wait` as a dead
  role. Recover a builder's liveness from *OpenCode builder session id* with
  `scripts/oc.sh --status <id>`: `active` means still running — wait, do not
  abort. Never the v1 `GET /session/status`; it does not exist on v2.
- When the canonical flow says to dispatch a Claude `reviewer`
  (`reviewer_model: claude/…`), run it headlessly: write the brief (what the
  reviewer cannot know from disk) to `.pipeline/T-<id>.review-<N>.brief`, then
  `scripts/bg-dispatch.sh start T-<id> review-<N> -- scripts/claude-review.sh
  T-<id> <N> .pipeline/T-<id>.review-<N>.brief`. The reply lands in
  `.pipeline/T-<id>.review-<N>.out`; branch on its `VERDICT:` line and paste it
  under the canonical `### Pass N — <date> — verdict: <V>` heading.
  `verdict=MISSING` is a failed dispatch, never a pass. The `claude` CLI
  reaches Anthropic and writes under `~/.claude`, so it needs the same narrow
  runtime approval as `oc.sh`, and it may only send what the project's
  guidance file authorizes for review.
- Stopping a role on purpose — the user says stop, or switches model or
  implementer — is `scripts/oc.sh --interrupt <id>`, then stopping the
  wrapper, then `--status` to confirm. Follow the canonical *mid-task model or
  implementer switch* steps for the rest.
- A status question ("where did the team stop?", "what's next?") runs the
  canonical *Where the team stopped* section and nothing else: report and
  stop; never dispatch from a status question. A task another lead parked for
  you names you in its handoff (`next: a Codex-led session dispatches the
  tester`) — pick it up from that line and the Decisions log, not from memory.
- You have no structured question tool. A stop-and-ask is one message: the
  question, numbered options with the recommended one first and marked, one
  line each on what happens next — then stop and wait. Never fold a question
  into a progress report where it can be skimmed past.
- Read every role's claim about the environment ("another dispatch is writing
  my files", "the server is down") as a lead to check, not a fact; the
  canonical *concurrent writer* paragraph names the checks.
- Update the status board every time a task's Status changes, not only at
  the end, so a lead from another vendor resuming the task reads the same
  board. After a merge you are told about, write the canonical merge record
  before starting anything else.
- Load the installed `delegate`, `karpathy-guidelines`, and `status-board`
  repository skills when the canonical flow calls for them.
- Do not replace the state-file handoff with Codex subagent chat context. Read
  `Latest handoff` between steps and use the structural scripts at the exact
  gates named by the canonical flow.
- OpenCode auto mode applies only to OpenCode dispatch. It never authorizes
  a Codex permission change. Spec approval and merge consent are workflow
  gates; runtime approval for sandbox/network/Git access is a separate control.
  At the approved merge step, request runtime approval for the precise Git
  command if needed. Configuration updates under `.codex/` or `.agents/skills/`
  may also need runtime approval; never move those files to evade protection.
- At a restart or compaction, recover the active task from `.pipeline/`, read
  its status, counters, approvals in the Decisions log, and Latest handoff,
  and verify existing worker activity before dispatching. Do not repeat an
  approval already recorded, and do not infer approval from silence.
- On a quota failure, preserve the task and thread ids and report the failing
  step. Retry through an available scheduled wakeup only; if this runtime
  cannot schedule one, state that limitation and leave a resumable handoff.
  Never claim a retry has been scheduled when no mechanism exists.

If the Codex planner cannot be spawned, stop and tell the user; do not silently
plan and implement in the lead thread.
